# Aberrations and Lens Design
#
# Refraction, dispersion, the five Seidel aberrations, and coatings as sourced claims, and the photographic lens from Petzval in 1840 through the double-Gauss line that includes the Helios 44-2.

#>optics_aberrations
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!optics
#:form #!ledger
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bone
#:era #!industrial

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Aberrations and Lens Design"
 ~#claim: "A lens trades five monochromatic aberrations and two chromatic ones against each other; field curvature is set by the Petzval sum of 1/(n f), which bending cannot change."
 ~#proof: "Smith and Born and Wolf for the aberration theory; Hecht for refraction and coatings; Kingslake for the design history; Rashed for Ibn Sahl."
 ~#door: "Image a window with a magnifying glass to see field curvature, then read the Petzval claim."
 ~#audience: "photographers, cinematographers, and physics students who want to know why lenses look the way they do"
 continuity: "Aberrations and Lens Design | Petzval sum"
 cold_open: "Every lens is a negotiated truce among its aberrations, and the Petzval sum decides how flat its focus can be."
}

